# The built plugin is tracked on purpose, and merging it is not.
#
# #171 measured the cost of keeping `main.js` in the tree: an unresolvable
# conflict in a minified file between any two branches that both built, seen on
# 2026-08-30 and repeatedly since. Git was trying to merge 2.8 MB of generated
# output line by line, and every such merge is meaningless — the file is not
# authored, it is produced.
#
# `-merge` stops git attempting that. A conflicting bundle is reported and left
# alone rather than turned into spliced garbage, and the resolution is always
# the same one command:
#
#     npm run build
#
# `-diff` keeps `git diff` and `git log -p` from printing megabytes of minified
# JavaScript that nobody reads.
#
# What this does NOT do is untrack them. The root bundle is what lets a user
# install the plugin straight from the repository, and `releases/*/main.js` is
# the same capability for older versions — the `.gitignore` rule that excludes
# `main.js` and then re-includes `releases/*/main.js` is deliberate, not an
# oversight. Neither CI workflow reads the committed file; both build their own
# and attach the result to a release.
main.js -diff -merge
styles.css -diff -merge
releases/*/main.js -diff -merge
releases/*/styles.css -diff -merge
